(6040799, 887564, 52336, '<td valign="top">That''s neat.  The HDR isn''t my cup of tea, and the light''s a tad harsh at the shadow, but it works elsewhere.  Different self-portrait, nicely done. 7</td>', 'That''s neat.  The HDR isn''t my cup of tea, and the light''s a tad harsh at the shadow, but it works elsewhere.  Different self-portrait, nicely done. 7', '2010-06-04 14:34:55.000000', NULL, 1),
(6040809, 887712, 52336, '<td valign="top">A Mason ring, interesting.  I think the light is the issue with this portrait.  The most well-lit element is your hand, and I consider that a mistake.  It should be your eyes in my view, which are the least well-lit here.  The position of your face is affecting where the light falls, or, if this is flash or studio, the lights are not positioned ideally.  The right eye is completely "dead" -- not a hint of a catchlight, those areas of reflected light in the eyes that give portraits so much depth, and help the viewer connect with the subject.  Lastly, the border is a bit too much -- it does indeed affect the experience of the viewer, and something more restrained, perhaps even coloured subtly to reflect the colours in the photo, would have been ideal.  I can see you''ve tried, though, and so 6.</td>', 'A Mason ring, interesting.  I think the light is the issue with this portrait.  The most well-lit element is your hand, and I consider that a mistake.  It should be your eyes in my view, which are the least well-lit here.  The position of your face is affecting where the light falls, or, if this is flash or studio, the lights are not positioned ideally.  The right eye is completely "dead" -- not a hint of a catchlight, those areas of reflected light in the eyes that give portraits so much depth, and help the viewer connect with the subject.  Lastly, the border is a bit too much -- it does indeed affect the experience of the viewer, and something more restrained, perhaps even coloured subtly to reflect the colours in the photo, would have been ideal.  I can see you''ve tried, though, and so 6.', '2010-06-04 14:39:33.000000', NULL, 1),
(6040816, 887357, 52336, '<td valign="top">I get what you''re doing, but it''s the attention to detail that makes a portrait like this, and you''ve missed the potential to really, really grab the viewer with the eyes.  I think the background colour is well done, but others may see it as too much because, conversely, it''s not enough.  There''s a portrait of Jeff Goldblum with these exact colours -- a purple background and overall light with an orange gel filter -- that really works, because the colours are bold and impressive.  Here, the purple is not forceful enough, and comes off as something of an afterthought, or even an error.  6</td>', 'I get what you''re doing, but it''s the attention to detail that makes a portrait like this, and you''ve missed the potential to really, really grab the viewer with the eyes.  I think the background colour is well done, but others may see it as too much because, conversely, it''s not enough.  There''s a portrait of Jeff Goldblum with these exact colours -- a purple background and overall light with an orange gel filter -- that really works, because the colours are bold and impressive.  Here, the purple is not forceful enough, and comes off as something of an afterthought, or even an error.  6', '2010-06-04 14:42:31.000000', NULL, 1),
(6040826, 887636, 52336, '<td valign="top">Some things work: the light''s okay, the colours are interesting (green is a risky choice but works here), catchlights are done nicely.  Overall, it''s not as strong a portrait for me because of the odd pose (a viewer-left facing portrait can work, but the pose must be immaculate), and the sense that I don''t get anything from this other than "yearbook photo" or "family photo".  I can''t tell what''s going on with you here, and that''s extremely important.  Some of the entrants really nail this (look for the kid with the guitar), some don''t get it too well.  I just get the sense of a guy with a green shirt and a smile.  That''s not so bad, and as I say the portrait is okay overall, but it hasn''t made me want to stop and consider it for very long. 6</td>', 'Some things work: the light''s okay, the colours are interesting (green is a risky choice but works here), catchlights are done nicely.  Overall, it''s not as strong a portrait for me because of the odd pose (a viewer-left facing portrait can work, but the pose must be immaculate), and the sense that I don''t get anything from this other than "yearbook photo" or "family photo".  I can''t tell what''s going on with you here, and that''s extremely important.  Some of the entrants really nail this (look for the kid with the guitar), some don''t get it too well.  I just get the sense of a guy with a green shirt and a smile.  That''s not so bad, and as I say the portrait is okay overall, but it hasn''t made me want to stop and consider it for very long. 6', '2010-06-04 14:46:46.000000', NULL, 1),
(6040867, 887695, 52336, '<td valign="top">You forgot the T and added an extra L. ;-)  Nice portrait -- the light''s well done, and seems nicely layered, but, and it''s a small point, your pose is too "Vogue" to be thoughtful.  In my opinion, square crop doesn''t work for this photo -- it seems to demand a standard portrait aspect ratio, despite what your right arm is doing.  For square crop to work, you''d have had to really work your pose.  In my view, the lines don''t invite a square crop.  For a portrait where a square crop really works, check yanko''s "automne".  The lines there complement the frame, and you''d have had to pull something like that off to make it work here. 6</td>', 'You forgot the T and added an extra L. ;-)  Nice portrait -- the light''s well done, and seems nicely layered, but, and it''s a small point, your pose is too "Vogue" to be thoughtful.  In my opinion, square crop doesn''t work for this photo -- it seems to demand a standard portrait aspect ratio, despite what your right arm is doing.  For square crop to work, you''d have had to really work your pose.  In my view, the lines don''t invite a square crop.  For a portrait where a square crop really works, check yanko''s "automne".  The lines there complement the frame, and you''d have had to pull something like that off to make it work here. 6', '2010-06-04 15:11:17.000000', NULL, 1),
(6040871, 887687, 52336, '<td valign="top">Your portrait doesn''t work because you haven''t paid enough attention to the light -- it''s straight on, as indicated by the overall flatness of the light and lack of contrast in your face, and the position of the catchlight in your visible eye.  Although such light sometimes works, it''s tricky professionals'' work.  You''d have been best served by a standard portrait setup with lights at either of the two 45 degree angles to your face and appropriately powered, or, if you have no such setup, by soft natural light coming through a window and highlighting one side of your face, with the other helped by a reflector.  The background is much too dark, and your top blends into it in an uninteresting way. 5</td>', 'Your portrait doesn''t work because you haven''t paid enough attention to the light -- it''s straight on, as indicated by the overall flatness of the light and lack of contrast in your face, and the position of the catchlight in your visible eye.  Although such light sometimes works, it''s tricky professionals'' work.  You''d have been best served by a standard portrait setup with lights at either of the two 45 degree angles to your face and appropriately powered, or, if you have no such setup, by soft natural light coming through a window and highlighting one side of your face, with the other helped by a reflector.  The background is much too dark, and your top blends into it in an uninteresting way. 5', '2010-06-04 15:14:14.000000', NULL, 1),
(6040875, 887677, 52336, '<td valign="top">The light is straight on -- one can tell by the catchlights in your eyes and the complete lack of contrast in your face.  It makes for a very uninteresting portrait (not helped by your total lack of expression).  Light to the side, or natural light through a large window to one side of you, would have greatly enhanced this, as would a smile, or a side glance, or an upward look taken from slightly above, or anything but such a prosaic dead-on shot. 4</td>', 'The light is straight on -- one can tell by the catchlights in your eyes and the complete lack of contrast in your face.  It makes for a very uninteresting portrait (not helped by your total lack of expression).  Light to the side, or natural light through a large window to one side of you, would have greatly enhanced this, as would a smile, or a side glance, or an upward look taken from slightly above, or anything but such a prosaic dead-on shot. 4', '2010-06-04 15:16:21.000000', NULL, 1),
(6040877, 887627, 52336, '<td valign="top">Your portrait needs something of interest -- either an interesting use of the natural light, a clever (though still restrained) pose, something that tells us about what you are, etc.  The portrait is too soft, especially at the eyes, where a viewer connects.  The background is truly a mess.  It would have helped to be in the bokeh, or at least shot from a more interesting angle. 4</td>', 'Your portrait needs something of interest -- either an interesting use of the natural light, a clever (though still restrained) pose, something that tells us about what you are, etc.  The portrait is too soft, especially at the eyes, where a viewer connects.  The background is truly a mess.  It would have helped to be in the bokeh, or at least shot from a more interesting angle. 4', '2010-06-04 15:18:57.000000', NULL, 1),
(6040880, 886471, 52336, '<td valign="top">Neat, dramatic, different, nice duo/split, interesting location, wonderful frame/crop.  7</td>', 'Neat, dramatic, different, nice duo/split, interesting location, wonderful frame/crop.  7', '2010-06-04 15:19:47.000000', NULL, 1),
(6040885, 887726, 52336, '<td valign="top">Would have been a stunner but for that hard dark line to the right.  It just destroys the symmetry of this portrait in my view.  Otherwise, you have used what appears to be natural light beautifully and to great effect, unlike so many in this challenge struggling with their flashes, and this portrait absolutely begs for the soft treatment you''ve given it.  Your pose is touching, your lovely face enigmatic, the contrast dramatic for such an intimate portrait and therefore a curious piquancy.  Everything about this is just beautiful but for the line, but 8 from me despite it.</td>', 'Would have been a stunner but for that hard dark line to the right.  It just destroys the symmetry of this portrait in my view.  Otherwise, you have used what appears to be natural light beautifully and to great effect, unlike so many in this challenge struggling with their flashes, and this portrait absolutely begs for the soft treatment you''ve given it.  Your pose is touching, your lovely face enigmatic, the contrast dramatic for such an intimate portrait and therefore a curious piquancy.  Everything about this is just beautiful but for the line, but 8 from me despite it.', '2010-06-04 15:23:09.000000', NULL, 1),
(6040889, 887632, 52336, '<td valign="top">Could have been much more, had you paid attention to the light at the window and used it to soften this.  As it is, the details aren''t there, and the attention to them is lacking, and so 6.</td>', 'Could have been much more, had you paid attention to the light at the window and used it to soften this.  As it is, the details aren''t there, and the attention to them is lacking, and so 6.', '2010-06-04 15:24:41.000000', NULL, 1),
(6040891, 887719, 52336, '<td valign="top">Title, as in Neil Diamond?  I think you''ve used the rails to nice effect, and it''s unusual enough to be interesting. 7</td>', 'Title, as in Neil Diamond?  I think you''ve used the rails to nice effect, and it''s unusual enough to be interesting. 7', '2010-06-04 15:26:00.000000', NULL, 1),
